  """
// Biochemistry from Persistence Structure.
//
// Biochemistry is the chemistry of persistence at the molecular
// scale. Life is not a special category: it is the canopy of
// molecular configurations that maintain coherence through
// self-referential transformation. Living systems are shapes
// that include themselves in their own context (S in C) and
// actively maintain their own coherence.
//
// The bridge from chemistry to biochemistry:
//   Chemistry: which atoms bond and how.
//   Biochemistry: which molecular configurations self-maintain.
//
// Carbon is the structural backbone because:
//   Z=6, config [He]2s^2 2p^2. Four valence electrons.
//   Can form 4 covalent bonds (sp3) or mixed (sp2, sp).
//   Tetrahedral geometry (sp3) = maximum symmetry for 4 bonds.
//   The ONLY element that can form long stable chains with itself
//   while also bonding diverse other elements. Silicon (Z=14)
//   is similar but larger radius makes Si-Si bonds weaker and
//   Si-O bonds too strong (locks into silicates).
//
// Water is the structural medium because:
//   H2O: bent geometry (sp3 on O, 2 bonding + 2 lone pairs).
//   Polar: O is electronegative (EN=3.44), H is not (EN=2.20).
//   Hydrogen bonding: the lone pairs on O attract H on other
//   molecules. This creates a liquid with high heat capacity,
//   surface tension, and solvent capacity.
//   Water's polarity makes it the universal solvent for ions
//   and polar molecules: the medium in which biochemistry occurs.
//
// Chirality in biochemistry inherits from physics:
//   The Higgs defines forward chirality globally.
//   Amino acids are almost all L-form (left-handed).
//   Sugars are almost all D-form (right-handed).
//   This is not random: it traces to the same SU(2) chirality
//   that produces baryogenesis. The weak force bias propagates
//   through molecular selection.
//
// Derives from: theory.chemistry.bonding, theory.chemistry.properties,
//               theory.physics.chirality
  """
}
